 ## Overview
-L2G-NeRF is a bundle-adjusting method that jointly optimizes neural 3D representations and camera poses by combining a local photometric alignment objective with a global geometric alignment objective [S1]. It addresses the problem of recovering camera poses from poor initialization (e.g., identity transformation) where standard parametric methods like BARF diverge, and non-parametric methods fail to enforce geometric constraints [S1]. It sets itself apart by using a two-stage process: first, a pixel-wise flexible alignment via a warp neural field, and second, a frame-wise constrained parametric alignment using differentiable solvers [S1].
+L2G-NeRF is a bundle-adjusting method that jointly optimizes neural 3D representations and camera poses by combining a local photometric alignment objective with a global geometric alignment objective [S1]. It solves the problem of suboptimal solutions caused by poor initialization in parametric methods and the inability to handle large displacements in non-parametric methods [S1]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [BARF](6872-barf-bundle-adjusting-neural-radiance-fields.md) by computing gradients with respect to local pixel-wise poses rather than global poses, allowing it to tolerate larger noise perturbations before divergence [S1].
 
